node_modules
.next
out
# SQLite and its sidecars. The WAL holds recently written rows, so committing it
# publishes whatever was last written — including account emails and password hashes.
*.db
*.db-journal
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
# The whole env family, with the committed template as the single exception. This is a
# denylist turned into an allowlist on purpose: the previous rules named the shapes anyone
# had thought of — .env.local, .env.local.bak — and the sync client promptly invented one
# nobody had. It duplicated `.env.example` as `.env 2.example` in the published copy, which
# means it inserts its number BEFORE what it reads as the extension, so a duplicate of the
# real secrets file is `.env 2.local`. That matched none of the old rules and none of the
# extension-scoped rules below, because `.local` is not among them.
#
# Nothing had leaked when this was found — only .env.example has ever been committed here.
# But this repository already had its history scrubbed once for a file variant the ignore
# rules did not anticipate, and enumerating variants is what failed both times. Ignore
# everything and name the one file that is allowed out.
.env*
!.env.example
.DS_Store
*.tsbuildinfo

# Sync-client duplicates: "index 2.ts", "page 3.tsx".
#
# 164 of these once sat untracked in the published AI_apps copy and 54 were swept into a
# commit by `git add -A`, turning 56 files into 110. Deleting them does NOT hold: they come
# back carrying their ORIGINAL mtimes, hours older than the deletion, which is a sync client
# replaying its copy rather than a build regenerating one. Ignoring them is what holds.
#
# Scoped to source and config extensions, which covered all 164 observed cases, rather than
# a blanket `* [0-9].*`. The blanket rule would also hide an asset named "Figure 1.png", and
# it would hide it SILENTLY — a junk file with an unexpected extension merely showing up as
# untracked is the far better failure. No tracked file in this repo has a space in its name.
* [0-9].ts
* [0-9].tsx
* [0-9].js
* [0-9].mjs
* [0-9].cjs
* [0-9].json
* [0-9].md
* [0-9].css
* [0-9].patch
# Extensionless duplicates: the same client produced a bare `.gitignore 2`. Safe to match
# broadly in a way `* [0-9].*` is not — the asset that rule would have hidden silently,
# "Figure 1.png", has an extension by definition, and a real file called "Figure 1" with
# none is not a thing this repository contains.
* [0-9]
